## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Sampling distributions)
## DBsection(Sample mean)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## Level(1)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('11')
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'parameter', 'sampling distribution', 'sample')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$n    = random(100, 300, 10);
@opts = (
    'is greater than',
    'is equal to',
    'is less than',
    'has not enough information'
);
$dd1 = DropDown([@opts], 1);
$dd2 = DropDown([@opts], 2);

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
BEGIN_PGML

A study on the length of time a person brushes their teeth is conducted on a
large population of adults. The mean brushing time is [` \mu `] and the standard
deviation is [` \sigma `].  A simple random sample of [$n] adults is considered.

a) The mean of the sampling distribution [_]{$dd1} the mean of the population.

b) The standard deviation of the sampling distribution [_]{$dd2} the standard deviation of the population.
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
These questions are basics of sample distributions.  
a) The mean of any sampling distribution is equal to the mean of the population.
b) Recall that the standard deviation of the sampling distribution is 
    [``` \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}```]
    and for any [`n>1`], this number is *less than* the population standard deviation [`\mu`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.